    {
      "role": "user",
      "content": "hey so i'm working with some high quality dark chocolate and i'm getting this weird white stuff on the surface after it sets. it looks like a dull film or streaks. is that just sugar or is it something else? how do i fix the tempering so it doesn't happen again?",
      "source": "evaluator"
    },
    {
      "role": "assistant",
      "content": "The white film or streaks you're seeing on your dark chocolate are likely a sign of blooming, which occurs when cocoa butter migrates to the surface of tempered dark chocolate, creating a dull, waxy appearance. To prevent blooming, ensure proper tempering and handling of your chocolate. This can be achieved by tempering the chocolate to the ideal working temperature (around 82°F to 86°F), then cooling it slowly and not over-working the chocolate. Regular tempering and storage in a cool, dry place can also help prevent blooming.",
